<h2>From collaboration to four decades of litigation</h2>
<p>A 1986 technical collaboration agreement (amended in 1987) obliged Neycer to provide technical assistance for GMB&rsquo;s ceramics plant. Alleging delay and breach, GMB invoked arbitration in 1989, quantifying its claim at Rs. 2 crore in the correspondence by which it nominated its arbitrator. The Umpire&rsquo;s award of 23 June 1999 granted GMB Rs. 11,69,62,910 with 15% interest — nearly six times the referred claim. A Single Judge of the Calcutta High Court (2015) limited the award to Rs. 2 crore; the Division Bench (2018) restored the full award; the Supreme Court has now restored the Single Judge.</p>

<h2>The jurisdictional architecture</h2>
<p>The judgment&rsquo;s organising principle is as old as arbitration law: <em>&ldquo;an arbitrator or umpire is a creature of the contract and the reference.&rdquo;</em> The tribunal&rsquo;s authority is what the parties referred — no more. Three consequences followed on these facts. The pecuniary ceiling fixed by GMB&rsquo;s own reference correspondence bound the umpire; as the Court held, <em>&ldquo;GMB could not unilaterally enlarge the scope of the reference by a subsequent letter&rdquo;</em> reserving rights. The Schedule C damages — projected brand and advertising losses resting on an unproved 1990 estimate — were speculative and fell independently. And clause 20 of the agreement, by which Neycer undertook earnest efforts toward commissioning, was not a guarantee insuring five years of GMB&rsquo;s profits and loan servicing; misconstruing it inflated the award further.</p>
<p>Two procedural holdings complete the picture. Contesting the merits before the umpire did not waive the jurisdictional objection — excess of reference can be raised in the challenge notwithstanding participation. And the valid portion of the award, up to Rs. 2 crore, was severable and sustained, with Neycer&rsquo;s Rs. 4 crore deposit treated as full and final satisfaction.</p>

<h2>Why a 1940 Act case matters in 2026</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>The reference is the jurisdiction.</strong> Under Section 34(2)(a)(iv) of the 1996 Act, an award dealing with disputes beyond the submission remains assailable — the quantification in the invocation notice and statement of claim defines the battlefield.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Quantify with care.</strong> A claimant who caps its claim in the reference correspondence may be held to that cap; amendments to claims must travel through the tribunal, not through unilateral letters.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Projections are not proof.</strong> Damages need evidence through the maker of the estimate; audited actuals will outweigh optimistic forecasts.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Severance saves the valid core.</strong> Courts prefer trimming the excess to annulling the whole where the parts are genuinely separable — the mirror image of the inseparability that defeated partial setting aside in other recent rulings.</div>
</div>
<div class="note">Legacy arbitrations under the 1940 Act still surface in execution and challenge proceedings decades on; their doctrines on reference, jurisdiction and severability continue to inform the 1996 Act regime. The financial directions in this judgment are specific to its record.</div>
